    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The NCAA has contended for more than a century that its revered tradition of amateurism carves out a special legal status for its “student-athletes.” In recent years a bevy of litigation on antitrust and labor law grounds has steadily eroded that long held position. Already, college athletics has experienced significant change, with a steady increase in the economic rights of college athletes. The next debate will be over the employment status of these athletes. This paper considers that debate as a question of law. We discuss the National Labor Relations Act and the Fair Labor Standards Act, as applied in current and past cases before the National Labor Relations Board and in federal court. We then consider the concept of players unions in college athletics and discuss implications for tax liability and Title IX compliance. We conclude that under the different standards of the NLRA and FLSA, some college athletes should be considered employees— especially those in the most heavily commercialized sports of Autonomy conference football and basketball—and some should not. Critically, we demonstrate the difficulty of differentiating between which are employees and which are not, given the arbitrary nature of the relevant legal standards and the heterogeneity of the day-to-day realities of college athletes across sports, schools, and levels of competition.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Anderson, L. (2024). The Employment Status of College Athletes. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/kg462ky4635. https://doi.org/10.25740/kg462ky4635.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
